From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kan Zhu <kanzhu@cs.washington.edu>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 06:14:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(alignment): decompose API-side request latency

The v2 records cover EngineCore only. A request's wall-clock latency as the
client sees it also contains the API frontend: generator activation, add_request,
the output collector handoff, and SSE serialization. Alignment had no visibility
into any of it, so the simulator had nothing to compare its client-side queueing
against.

Adds a v3 `VibeSimAlignmentApiRequestTiming` record on the completions and disagg
streaming paths, splitting that span into frontend prepare / stream activation /
add_request / collector wait / engine output wait / output fanout / collector
wakeup / generator resume / first-output serialize, plus the token receive and
SSE yield spans and the terminal tail.

EngineCore and the API frontend are separate processes, so their monotonic
clocks share no origin. `build_alignment_api_timing_record` therefore only ever
subtracts within one domain -- the EngineCore TTFT and decode durations are
carried alongside as their own values, never differenced against an API
timestamp. That is what the new unit test pins, by passing the two domains
deliberately different origins.

Five API-process timestamps go on `RequestStateStats`. They are stamped from the
loops that already run (`RequestOutputCollector.put`, the per-output loop in
`process_outputs`) rather than from a new pass, and each is guarded by a
`== 0.0` first-write check plus a non-empty `token_ids` check so prompt-only and
empty outputs do not claim the first-token boundary.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
